{"ratings":[{"id":"C1","consequence":"wrong","trigger":"plausible","reason":"Replacing or recreating a rejected model leaves the corrected backend unavailable until the stale block expires or the daemon restarts."},{"id":"C2","consequence":"wrong","trigger":"plausible","reason":"An operator cannot clear an existing default effort through PATCH, which can leave the model unusable for some roles."},{"id":"C3","consequence":"outage","trigger":"plausible","reason":"Deleting a model referenced by the file-backed policy succeeds but prevents the daemon from starting on its next restart."},{"id":"C4","consequence":"wrong","trigger":"plausible","reason":"An older overlapping probe can overwrite newer discovery results and cause routing to skip a served model."},{"id":"C5","consequence":"outage","trigger":"plausible","reason":"Promoting a persisted runtime model into configuration or removing its provider can prevent the daemon from starting."},{"id":"C8","consequence":"test","trigger":"plausible","reason":"Slow fixture startup can cause retries to add process IDs before the cleanup test counts them, producing a test failure without a runtime effect."},{"id":"C9","consequence":"wrong","trigger":"plausible","reason":"Evaluations can invoke a model already known to be unserved, consuming an attempt and potentially recording an avoidable failure."},{"id":"C10","consequence":"wrong","trigger":"plausible","reason":"A queued chat request can invoke a model after discovery reports that its provider no longer serves it."},{"id":"C5b","consequence":"outage","trigger":"plausible","reason":"Deleting a model referenced by the file-backed policy can prevent the daemon from restarting until the policy or catalog is repaired."},{"id":"C6b","consequence":"diag","trigger":"plausible","reason":"Smoke checks omit disabled providers without reporting them as skipped, reducing report detail for an operator who later re-enables one."}]}